a  kind of environmental remediation  program so it has to be a larger  synthetic project would be my argument  of taking into account all of these  other constituencies and conditions as  opposed to really just thinking about  how do you take the pollution out of the  watering and are they receptive to those  ideas they've definitely been very  receptive I think that it's always  tricky coming you know added from a long  long long term view versus the way in  which I think a lot of members of  government and politicians operate which  is in the you know in an election cycle  as opposed to five or ten year plan so I  think a lot of times there's in search  of a silver bullet that will fix these  things but it took a long time to get  the way that it has and I think it will  also take of course a long time to try  to make improvements to it so that might  happen in a piecemeal level but I'm  hopeful I remain hopeful that it might  be holistically tackled by the  government and and various  national and state government and also  non-government organization right  hopefully that will happen so while  you're here yeah I know you're working  on a project done does it tie into this  what what are your next steps definitely  so the work that I'm doing here at Yale  is working on my my next book and an  exhibition that looks at the history of  village India really from Independence  all the way through the kind of middle  to late period of the Cold War and I  came across a new village in India  because having walked so much of the  Ganges I was always amazed by the amount  of village just the actual density  villages that existed here and it's  oftentimes been historic sail'd projects  to develop villages and I was very  interested in how the issues surrounding  villages were very much tied to  environmental questions of how do you  develop better agriculture sanitation  how do you improve the larger  environment not just physically but also  socially and that was very much tied to  a lot of the work that I started to  study with the history of tubewells and  decentralization in India so my next  book is looking at the history of  decentralization and village India and  then a series of exhibitions that look  at the modes of trying to develop  pedagogy visual pedagogy for villagers  in India during the Cold War to try to  teach them or persuade them on how to  make improvements to their villages to  be part of a larger decentralized  nation-building project  excellent we will look forward to that  new book coming out okay thank you for  being here with us today thank you for  having me for more information about  professor at Giovanni and his work  please visit our website at macmillan  report yale.edu be sure to join us again  for another episode of the Macmillan  report made possible through funding  from the Whitney and Eddie Macmillan  Center for International and area  studies at you  [Music]
 

